---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const q = [
  ['1', 'What were Ivone\'s parents\' given names?', 'Their surnames are known — the 1948 divorce file has her born MOTA FIGUEIREDO — but the given names are still blank, and every paper route has been blocked. A surname sweep on 14 September 2026 seemed to open a new front door — 27 Figueiredo matches, 13 Mota — and it was withdrawn the same evening: the Figueiredo tag pairs her with a woman born before 1713, and MyHeritage states that the Mota matches share no ancestral surname with Derrick at all.', 'Her Porto death record, which names both parents outright, and the 1924 Lourenço Marques registration. On the DNA side the useful matches remain the two already worked — 108.7 cM at second cousin\'s son, and 133.2 cM — whose families carry the surnames Jorge, Silvestre, Conde, Gaspar and Matos.', 'Ivone'],
  ['2', 'Who was Derrick\'s biological father?', 'The DNA excludes Kurt Israel Luwinski and points to an English family with a South African connection. The Y-DNA was run on 14 September 2026 and agreed: R-S11477, R1b branch U106/Z381, the North Sea lineage — whose largest European population is England. Two systems, one direction. The exclusion is settled; the name is not.', 'NARROWED TO EIGHT NAMES, 15 September 2026. His biological grandparent was a sibling of Walter George Brown or of Agnes Kate Castle, and both sets are now named from the match\'s own tree of 10,715 people: Archibald Alfred Brown 1879-1959, Frank Henry Brown b.1880, Alice Brown b.1884, Ernest H Brown 1897-1972; George W Castle b.1886, Ada Castle b.1887, Frederick Castle b.1890, Martha M Castle b.1896. A Norfolk family — Norwich, Great Yarmouth, Caister. Three of the eight are daughters, whose sons would carry a husband\'s surname, and the closest match of all is a HALSEY whose shared ancestral place with Derrick is South Africa. Confirmed in the same tree: a Brown daughter, Decima Audrey Harrison 1928-1997, which is where the Harrison match comes from. Earlier, 14 September:  cross-match centimorgans place the cluster as one line read at two depths — the closest match at his grandparents, the 10,715-person tree at his great-grandparents, and those two third cousins to each other exactly as that predicts. Surnames HALSEY (grandparent depth, shared place South Africa) and BROWN/CASTLE/WEBB (great-grandparent depth, all named and dead). His biological father was a first cousin of Geoffrey David Brown b.1926 or Hazel Webb b.1931.', 'Write to the two match holders — one is the closest, one holds the big tree — and ask about a cousin who went to South Africa, about a Halsey marriage, and about the grandfather her tree records only as Unknown. Not the Y, first. R-S11477\'s common ancestor lived about 200 BCE, so the surnames on that branch — Ballew, Stafford, Blakely, Byrd, York — are ninety generations away, and testing all five against his match list returned nothing above 28 cM. The close autosomal matches are far nearer the answer: the Halsey match is his first cousin\'s daughter at 297.5 cM, so her common ancestors with him are his own biological grandparents, and that grandfather\'s surname is in her tree. NEXT, and it needs nobody\'s permission: look for a marriage of Alice Brown (b.1884 Norwich), Ada Castle (b.1887) or Martha M Castle (b.1896) to a man named HALSEY in the England and Wales marriage index — one entry would join the tree on one side to the surname on the other. Four replies went to the matches themselves on 15 September. Then Big Y-700, which would place him on one of the 42 branches below S11477 and open FamilyTreeDNA\'s Y match list.', 'The DNA'],
  ['3', 'When did Auguste divorce Jacob, and why?', 'The tree records the divorce with no date. In a Berlin Mischehe the date is the whole meaning of the act.', 'Berlin-Schöneberg divorce files; the 5 January 1924 marriage entry that preceded them.', 'East Prussia'],
  ['4', 'Where does Kurt\'s "Israel" come from?', 'He left Germany in 1933, six years before the compulsory-name decree, and as a Mischling would have been outside it anyway.', 'German expatriation lists in the Reichsanzeiger; South African naturalisation and alien registration papers.', 'Kurt'],
  ['5', 'Is Georg a brother or a cousin?', 'Born at Hammerstein two months after Jacob was born at Schubin. Both dates from the Bundesarchiv. They cannot both be sons of one mother.', 'ASKED, 15 September 2026. Both towns are now in Poland and both civil registries opened in 1874, so an 1884 birth entry names both parents outright. Written to Archiwum Państwowe w Bydgoszczy for Standesamt Schubin, and to Archiwum Państwowe w Koszalinie for Standesamt Hammerstein and any Jewish register covering Sally Leibholz, born there in 1847. If both entries survive, one line of each decides it.', 'The Leibholz brothers'],
  ['6', 'What happened to Gertrud Leibke on 27 February 1943?', 'She died in Berlin, not in a camp, on the day of the Fabrikaktion. The tree also duplicates her across two brothers.', 'Berlin death register; merge the duplicate and establish which brother she married.', 'The Leibholz brothers'],
  ['7', 'When did Ivone actually marry de Witt?', 'Recorded as 2 February 1948, four and a half months before she married Kurt, with no divorce between.', 'The Germiston marriage register.', 'Ivone'],
  ['8', 'Who was the Pinto da Rocha?', 'Henrique\'s father, and the tree can only call him "Unknown da Rocha". LOCATED, 14 September 2026: Henrique\'s South African deceased estate, gazetted three times in 2023 — estate 12294/2023, DA ROCHA, HENRIQUE FIGUEIREDO PINTO. A South African estate file contains the Death Notice, form J294, and that form asks for the full names of the father and the maiden name of the mother.', 'Order the J294 from the Master of the High Court quoting 12294/2023. Then his 1968 marriage record, which unlike the 1948 form does carry parents. And in parallel a letter to the Arquivo da Defesa Nacional: he turned twenty five weeks after the Colonial War began, and a Portuguese military registration names both parents.', 'Ivone'],
  ['9', 'Does Jacob have a Stolperstein?', 'None found in Schöneberg. The Leibholz stones located so far belong to other Leibholz families.', 'Koordinierungsstelle Stolpersteine Berlin. If there is none, there could be.', 'The Leibholz brothers'],
  ['10', 'Do any of the 100 index Luwinskis connect?', 'Not one has been shown to. They may be entirely unrelated people who share a rare surname.', 'Allenstein and Kreis Rössel Catholic registers, for Joachim born 1865 and back.', 'The register'],
];
const href = { 'Ivone': '/ivone/', 'The DNA': '/dna/', 'East Prussia': '/east-prussia/', 'Kurt': '/kurt/', 'The Leibholz brothers': '/leibholz/', 'The register': '/register/' };
---
<Base title="Open questions"
  subtitle="Ten things this archive cannot answer yet, in the order they are worth attacking"
  lede="An archive is worth what its unanswered questions are worth. These are written so that anyone — a cousin, a stranger, an archivist — can pick one up and work it without asking permission.">

  {q.map(([n, question, state, next, page]) => (
    <div class="panel">
      <h3>{n}. {question}</h3>
      <p>{state}</p>
      <p class="small"><strong>Next:</strong> {next}</p>
      <p class="small"><a href={base + href[page]}>{page} →</a></p>
    </div>
  ))}

  <h2>Requests, drafted</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    Written out in full in <code>requests/</code> — the Porto one in Portuguese and English, ready to send.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conservatória do Registo Civil do Porto</strong> — death and burial of Ivone de Figueiredo, b. 4 March 1924 Lourenço Marques.</li>
    <li><strong>Arquivo Histórico de Moçambique</strong>, Maputo, and the <strong>Arquivo Histórico Ultramarino</strong>, Lisbon — Lourenço Marques civil registration, 1924 and c. 1941.</li>
    <li><strong>Landesarchiv Berlin</strong> — Schöneberg marriage 5 January 1924; the divorce file; Kurt's 1912 birth entry; Gertrud Leibke's death, 27 February 1943.</li>
    <li><strong>Arolsen Archives</strong> — Jacob, Samuel, Georg, Oskar, Elli and Werner Leibholz.</li>
    <li><strong>Master of the Supreme Court, Pretoria</strong> — estate file 7584/1969, Kurt Luwinski.</li>
    <li><strong>National Archives of South Africa</strong> — the 1948 and 1951 divorce actions, and the Germiston marriage register.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
